+
+## Declaring the Foreign Environment
+
+A foreign body runs against an interpreter and a set of installed packages, and
+Shape needs both written down. Declare them in `shape.toml` under
+`[foreign.<language>]`:
+
+```toml
+[foreign.python]
+runtime = "cpython"
+version = "3.11.7"
+root = ".venv"
+lockfile = "python.lock"
+```
+
+`runtime` and `version` pin the interpreter. `root` is the environment
+directory, relative to the project root — a virtualenv for Python. `lockfile`
+names the resolved package set; it defaults to `<language>.lock`.
+
+The lockfile is TOML:
+
+```toml
+version = 1
+language = "python"
+
+[packages.numpy]
+version = "1.26.4"
+integrity = "sha256-..."
+```
+
+Shape derives one **environment digest** from these declared, locked inputs —
+runtime identity, version, the lockfile's content, and the checker pin below.
+The digest is a property of what you wrote, never of the machine that compiled:
+the same source and the same lockfile produce the same digest on every host.
+Reordering or reformatting the lockfile does not change it; changing a package
+version does.
+
+### Nothing is discovered
+
+Shape does not look for a virtualenv. Earlier versions searched the working
+directory for `.venv/`, then `venv/`, then `pyrightconfig.json`, then
+`$VIRTUAL_ENV`, and activated whatever turned up — so the same program could
+import different packages depending on which directory you ran it from. That
+search is gone. A stray `.venv` next to your program has no effect, and neither
+does a `$VIRTUAL_ENV` inherited from your shell.
+
+The search paths come from the declaration instead:
+`lib/python3.11/site-packages` follows from `version = "3.11.7"`.
+
+If you declare no `[foreign.python]` at all, foreign bodies run against the base
+interpreter with nothing added to the import path.
+
+### A declared environment that is missing is an error
+
+If `[foreign.python]` names a `root` or a `lockfile` that this machine does not
+have, the first call into a `fn python` fails before the body is compiled:
+
+```
+foreign function 'correlation': [C0936] the `python` environment declares the
+root `/proj/.venv`, which is not a directory on this host. This is a pre-entry
+failure: nothing falls back to whatever the host happens to have installed.
+```
+
+The same applies to a missing lockfile, a lockfile written for another language,
+and a `root` whose layout disagrees with the declared version (a `python3.10`
+tree under a `version = "3.11.7"` declaration).
+
+### Pinning the checker
+
+The type checker that will verify foreign bodies is pinned alongside the
+environment, because its version and its strictness setting change its verdict
+as surely as a package version changes behaviour:
+
+```toml
+[foreign.python.checker]
+name = "pyright"
+version = "1.1.350"
+settings = { strict = true, pythonVersion = "3.11" }
+```
+
+The checker pin is part of the environment digest, so upgrading a checker is a
+reviewed change to your manifest rather than a build that breaks one morning
+because a tool updated underneath it. Compile-time checking of foreign bodies
+is not wired up yet — the pin is what makes it reproducible when it lands.
+
+### TypeScript modules
+
+TypeScript's environment declares modules rather than packages, and its lockfile
+maps each import specifier a body writes to a vendored file:
+
+```toml
+[foreign.typescript]
+runtime = "deno"
+version = "1.40.0"
+root = "vendor"
+```
+
+```toml
+version = 1
+language = "typescript"
+
+[modules."./stats"]
+path = "stats.js"
+integrity = "sha256-..."
+```
+
+A body imports it dynamically:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn typescript spread(xs: Vec<number>) -> Result<number> {
+    const stats = await import("./stats");
+    return stats.spread(xs);
+}
+```
+
+Resolution has exactly one source: this table. There is no `node_modules`
+search, no registry lookup, and no network. A specifier the lock does not
+declare is refused, and the refusal lists what the lock does declare.
+`integrity` is verified when present, so a vendored file that changed on disk is
+caught rather than executed.
+
+Three limits are worth knowing before you reach them:
+
+- Locks are written or generated by other tooling today. Shape does not yet turn
+  a `package.json` into a lockfile.
+- Vendored sources must be JavaScript. This runtime embeds V8, not the
+  TypeScript compiler; a `.ts` path is refused with pre-transpilation named as
+  the remedy.
+- A top-level `import` statement inside a fence is a syntax error — a foreign
+  body is a function body, not a module. Use dynamic `await import(...)`, as
+  above.
 
